from the sys package, the main.go file from the previous task, and checking that the sys module exists based on the implementation status. I need to think through what the stitch agent requires—the SRD itself, the differential testing approach, the SIGPIPE requirement, and the Go module structure with proper imports.\n\nFor the file paths, the SRD lives at `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd053-sort.yaml` and the use case at `docs/specs/use-cases/rel07.0-uc001-sort.yaml`. Now I'm reconsidering the task scope—the constraint limits me to 4 tasks maximum, and since all three rel07.0 utilities are unstarted with ready requirements, I need to decide whether to focus entirely on sort or distribute across multiple utilities. With 48 total requirements across the three, I can only cover a portion.\n\nStarting with sort makes the most sense: tackling 16 of its 19 requirements in 4 tasks gives meaningful progress on one utility rather than spreading thin across all three. The remaining sort requirements plus tr and tail would require additional batches, but this approach follows the principle of completing one thing first. I'm double-checking the constraint—it says \"Do NOT exceed 4 tasks,\" which overrides the general guideline of creating up to 10 tasks at a time.\n\nFor the file structure, task 0 will create cmd/sort/main.go while tasks 1-3 modify it, with each task listing the SRD in required_reading. I'm keeping the implementation to main.go for now rather than prescribing additional files—the stitch agent can decide if it needs to split things out further for organization.\n\nFor required_reading, I need to include the SRD, the use case, examples from pkg/sys/ for the SIGPIPE pattern, existing cmd/ packages as reference, and the test suite at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-07.0.yaml since it defines the expected behavior. Each subsequent task will also reference the code from the previous task.\n\nNow I'm writing out the actual task definitions. I'm looking at the ac